# What an Operating System Does

> The operating system (OS) is the program that turns a bag of hardware
> into a usable machine. It stands between applications — browsers,
> games, editors — and the CPU, memory, and devices underneath.

## 1. The Four Core Jobs

| Job | What it means | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Process management | Deciding which programs run, when, for how long | Playing music while browsing |
| Memory management | Giving each process its own memory, protecting them from each other | One crashed app doesn't corrupt another |
| File system | Turning raw disk blocks into files and folders | Saving a document |
| Device management | Talking to hardware through drivers | Printing, drawing to screen |

## 2. Processes

A **process** is a running program: its code plus its current state
(program counter, registers, open files, memory).

[definition]
**Multitasking** is the OS rapidly switching the CPU between processes.
The switch (**context switch**) happens thousands of times per second,
which creates the illusion of many programs running at once even on one
CPU core.
[/definition]

Processes move through states:

- **Ready** — waiting for a turn on the CPU
- **Running** — currently executing
- **Blocked** — waiting for something (disk read, network reply)

## 3. Virtual Memory

Each process sees its own private address space; the OS + hardware map
it onto physical RAM and, when RAM runs short, temporarily park less-used
pages on disk.

[important]
This isolation is why one misbehaving program usually cannot scribble on
another's memory. It is also why "more RAM than you have" seems possible:
**virtual memory** can exceed physical memory at the cost of speed.
[/important]

## 4. Files and Directories

- A **file system** organises data as files inside directories (folders).
- **Metadata** records size, timestamps, and permissions per file.
- **Permissions** decide who may read, write, or execute — the base of
  multi-user security.

## 5. Kernels and Modes

@diagram tree id="os-ring" layout="tree" aria="User mode versus kernel mode"
  "CPU modes" -> "User mode"
  "CPU modes" -> "Kernel mode"
  "Kernel mode" -> "Direct hardware access"
  "Kernel mode" -> "Runs drivers, scheduler, memory manager"
@/diagram

The **kernel** is the OS core. User programs run in *user mode* with
restricted rights; anything privileged (touching hardware) happens in
*kernel mode* via **system calls** — `open()`, `read()`, `fork()` and
friends.

[info title="Same principles, different kernels"]
Linux, Windows NT, and macOS's XNU all provide scheduling, memory
isolation, and files — they differ mainly in design choices (monolithic
vs hybrid kernels), not in the fundamentals you just learned.
[/info]

## Key Takeaways

- An OS manages processes, memory, files, and devices.
- Context switching creates multitasking; virtual memory creates isolation.
- System calls are the doorway from user programs into the kernel.
